{"type":"message","id":"56c023ff","parentId":"bfb3b6d4","timestamp":"2026-03-08T21:17:51.765Z","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"text","text":"[Sun 2026-03-08 22:17 GMT+1] Review the brain-inspired memory implementation roadmap for OpenClaw and identify which features would **really fit** the current architecture.\n\nContext: OpenClaw is an AI assistant with a neuro-memory-agent that implements human-inspired memory (see memory/2026-03-08-brain-vs-openclaw-comparison.md for full analysis).\n\nCurrent strengths:\n- Bayesian surprise (KL divergence, adaptive thresholds)\n- Two-stage retrieval (similarity + temporal expansion)\n- Forgetting curves (power-law decay)\n- Event mesh + predictive engine integration\n\nProposed additions (6 features):\n1. **Working Memory** - Prefrontal cortex analogue, ~7 active items\n2. **Precision Weighting** - Attention-modulated surprise\n3. **Emotional Valence** - Reward/importance tagging\n4. **Semantic Memory** - Separate store for abstracted facts\n5. **Sleep-Stage Consolidation** - Differentiated replay cycles\n6. **Hierarchical Segmentation** - Multi-scale event boundaries\n\nQuestion: Which features would genuinely complement OpenClaw's design philosophy? Consider:\n- Natural fit with event-driven architecture\n- Value for an AI assistant (not just research novelty)\n- Integration with existing systems (predictive engine, event mesh, MCP)\n- Practical benefits for users\n\nRead memory/2026-03-08-brain-vs-openclaw-comparison.md and recommend the 2-3 features that would have the biggest real-world impact."}],"timestamp":1773004671764}}
